			</div>
			<div class="content" id="post-content-1412020">
									

<p>The thing with super bugs is that their adaptations were actually genes already present in the bacteria, but the changes that made them more resistant to antibiotics make the bacteria &quot;less fit&quot; than non-resistant bacteria. With as many antibiotics we throw on pretty much everything, the resistance becomes worth it and becomes a dominant trait. In theory if we stopped using antibiotics for a decade, super bugs would get wiped out back to pre-antibiotic levels.. (Of course, the effects of using no antibiotics makes this a last resort option.)</p>
<p>What this translates to, is how long would whatever agency is making these things fire resistant keep it up if the Foundation started using an acid bath? Could they use a rotation of extermination devices to keep them under control?</p>

