														</div>
			</div>
			<div class="content" id="post-content-1445493">
									

<p>Hi, I'm pretty new here, but from my limited experience, I think certain parts of this article don't match foundation tone, and I'd like to offer my services as a copy-editor.</p>
<p>&quot;They <strong>often choose to attack</strong> with teeth or claws. Although fairly delicate <strong>(also, surprisingly, flammable)</strong>, they are reasonably strong and pose a considerable threat in large numbers.&quot;</p>
<p>1. I suggest &quot;They often attack with&#8230;&quot; or better yet &quot;They attack with&#8230;&quot;<br />
2. The parenthetical remark sounds conversational and thus deviates from the 'clinical, detached' tone of SCP articles. Also, since the vulnerability to fire seems to be important in the Addendum, it probably shouldn't be in parentheses anyway. I suggest &quot;Specimens/subjects/entities are delicate and flammable, but are strong enough to pose a serious threat in large numbers.</p>
<p>&quot;In addition, tipping or tilting SCP-019 will <strong>create a reaction as though it was previously</strong> “filled” with SCP-019-2 specimens&quot;</p>
<p>This sounds a little awkward, how about &quot;When tipped or tilted, SCP-019 will react as though it was/were filled with SCP-019-2 specimens&quot; - still sounds a little odd though.</p>
<p>&quot;The specimen <strong>only lived for under 48 hours</strong>, and a dissection revealed anatomy consistent on a cellular level with normal biology, but with an <strong>extremely unstable</strong> musculoskeletal structure. Other notable anomalies included an unstable respiratory system, <strong>next to no digestive tract, and virtually no other internal organs</strong>. All other captured specimens have followed similar patterns of behavior and demise.&quot;</p>
<p>1. Sounds a little dramatic, suggest &quot;The specimen survived for approximately 48 hours (or some more specific number).<br />
2. &quot;but with an unstable musculoskeletal structure.<br />
3. &quot;an incomplete digestive tract&quot;<br />
4 &quot;few additional internal organs&quot; or &quot;an absence of normal internal organs&quot;</p>
<p>I'm not sure if these are the sorts of edits I need permission to make, so I'll post this here. Aside from those issues the tone of the article looked fine to me. I like to concept, especially the idea that the specimens might originally have been citizens of Ancient Greece. +1</p>

							</div>

								
					
